#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/backup-emergency-restore-v4.rst:3
#: 037376021437442e85391c92c2e1d763
msgid "Emergency backup recovery (v4)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/backup-emergency-restore-v4.rst:6
#: 396ce34f2e714a73bd77db39ccc648b3
msgid ""
"This page describes how to perform an emergency restore of a backup created "
"on Qubes R4.X (which uses backup format version 4)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/backup-emergency-restore-v4.rst:8
#: 0f9fc7c94d174dc89abc2e257c3234ba
msgid ""
"The Qubes backup system is designed with emergency disaster recovery in "
"mind. No special Qubes-specific tools are required to access data backed up "
"by Qubes. In the event a Qubes system is unavailable, you can access your "
"data on any GNU/Linux system by following the instructions on this page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/backup-emergency-restore-v4.rst:10
#: 94713c3d9ddd4999a668c458b629538c
msgid ""
"**Important:** You may wish to store a copy of these instructions with your "
"Qubes backups. All Qubes documentation, including this page, is available in "
"plain text format in the `qubes-doc <https://github.com/QubesOS/qubes-"
"doc>`__ Git repository."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/backup-emergency-restore-v4.rst:13
#: 59cc70451b894351b92335277ddfdb0d
msgid "Required ``scrypt`` utility"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/backup-emergency-restore-v4.rst:16
#: 74dfbbc082e54ac5b9312ca12605a640
msgid ""
"In Qubes 4.X, backups are encrypted and integrity-protected with `scrypt "
"<https://www.tarsnap.com/scrypt.html>`__. You will need a copy of this "
"utility in order to access your data. Since ``scrypt`` is not pre-installed "
"on every GNU/Linux system, it is strongly recommended that you store a copy "
"of it with your backups. If your distribution has ``scrypt`` packaged (e.g., "
"Debian), you can install the package in the standard way using your "
"distribution’s package manager. Otherwise, you’ll need to obtain a compiled "
"binary (instructions below) or compile the program from source yourself. "
"(Don’t forget to :doc:`verify signatures </project-security/verifying-"
"signatures>` first!) Note that versions of ``scrypt`` up to 1.2.0 "
"(inclusive) do not support the ``-P`` option for easier scripting, which "
"means you’ll need to enter the passphrase for each file separately, instead "
"of using ``echo ... | scrypt``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/backup-emergency-restore-v4.rst:18
#: 406b1375382f47a7bc60766b2526740e
msgid ""
"Here are instructions for obtaining a compiled ``scrypt`` binary. This "
"example uses an RPM-based system (Fedora), but the same general procedure "
"should work on any GNU/Linux system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/backup-emergency-restore-v4.rst:20
#: 54ca052576ee42b3ac08e60c29811bf6
msgid ""
"If you’re not on Qubes 4.X, :ref:`import and authenticate the Release 4 "
"Signing Key <project-security/verifying-signatures:how to import and "
"authenticate release signing keys>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/backup-emergency-restore-v4.rst:28
#: b2c36872a7a24a94b020c3d965c3d420
msgid "Download the ``scrypt`` RPM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/backup-emergency-restore-v4.rst:35
#: b0725681ed8343fc99349350b55e6d9b
msgid "Or, if that doesn’t work:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/backup-emergency-restore-v4.rst:43
#: 28926d4a9b8548469df5d4537c2583b8
msgid "Verify the signature on the ``scrypt`` RPM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/backup-emergency-restore-v4.rst:51
#: 186cfee0c0574315a387d7ef8ec5aa41
msgid ""
"The message ``digests signatures OK`` means that both the digest (i.e., the "
"output of a hash function) and PGP signature verification were successful."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/backup-emergency-restore-v4.rst:53
#: 9a0db10544c14fa3b82cec15bd382243
msgid "Install ``rpmdevtools``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/how-to-guides/backup-emergency-restore-v4.rst:61
#: b9d7c8545abc43b8994e227a761ac3bc
msgid ""
"Extract the ``scrypt`` binary from the RPM and make it conveniently "
"available."
msgstr ""

